{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"delegable-persuasive-globefish","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Context\n\nFound by the `LLM-Review` bot on Gerrit change 2984 patchset 8 as three advisory (non-blocking) findings. They were deliberately kept out of that change's scope: folding review advisories back into 2984 is what made its earlier rounds diverge, so they are recorded here instead.\n\nAll three are in `infra/terraform/bedrock-spend-cap/handler.py`.\n\n## The three defects\n\n**1. `_ce_spend_today` raises `KeyError` on a no-data period (~line 354).**\nIt indexes `results[0]['Total']['UnblendedCost']['Amount']` whenever `ResultsByTime` is non-empty. Cost Explorer returns a period entry with an empty or absent `Total` for periods that have no data yet, so the index raises `KeyError`. A `KeyError` is not the `ClientError` the caller is guarding for, so it escapes the intended \"CE poll failed, fall back\" path and propagates as an unhandled exception. This is most likely immediately after the UTC day rolls, which is exactly when the cap logic matters.\n\n**2. The CE poll gate defeats its own rate limit during a metered outage (~line 493).**\nThe gate reads `if due or metered is None or ...`. When the CloudWatch metered signal is unavailable, `metered is None` is true on every tick, so a Cost Explorer call is forced every 5 minutes regardless of `CE_MIN_INTERVAL_SECONDS`. The rate limit is bypassed precisely during the outage window, when the poll is least likely to be productive and the extra calls are least affordable.\n\n**3. `DRY_RUN` release reports an attachment state that contradicts its own notification (~line 562).**\nWhen `DRY_RUN` is set and a release would occur, `_apply(attach=False)` is a no-op returning `[]`, but the handler's returned `deny_attached` calls `_is_attached()`, which re-reads live IAM and correctly reports `True`. The \"Bedrock daily cap released\" notification is published alongside that `True`. A dry run therefore emits a release announcement and a still-attached state in the same response, which is contradictory to anyone reading either signal.\n\n## Approach\n\nTDD, one defect at a time, each with an observed red test before the fix.\n\n1. For (1), a test drives `_ce_spend_today` against a Cost Explorer response whose single period carries an empty `Total`, and asserts the function reports no reading rather than raising. Decide explicitly whether \"no data yet\" is a zero reading or an unavailable signal, and make the caller's fallback path handle the chosen answer — this is the same distinction being drawn for `_metered_spend` on 2984, so the two should agree rather than each invent a convention.\n2. For (2), a test holds the metered signal unavailable across consecutive ticks inside `CE_MIN_INTERVAL_SECONDS` and asserts Cost Explorer is polled once, not once per tick. The fix must keep the outage from *suppressing* a due poll, so assert both directions.\n3. For (3), a test drives the dry-run release path and asserts the response and the notification agree. Decide which one is authoritative — most likely the dry run should report the state it would have produced, or should not announce a release it did not perform.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A test drives `_ce_spend_today` with a period entry carrying an empty `Total` and passes without raising `KeyError`. Verified by that test failing against the current code and passing after the fix.\n- [ ] A test asserts Cost Explorer is polled at most once within `CE_MIN_INTERVAL_SECONDS` while the metered signal is unavailable. Verified by counting poll calls across simulated consecutive ticks.\n- [ ] A test asserts a due CE poll still happens during a metered outage, so the rate-limit fix does not suppress polling entirely. Verified by advancing simulated time past the interval and observing the poll.\n- [ ] A test asserts the `DRY_RUN` release path's reported attachment state and its published notification do not contradict each other. Verified by that test failing against the current code.\n- [ ] Each new test fails when the production code it covers is deliberately broken. Verified by a recorded teeth check per test.\n- [ ] Branch coverage on `handler.py` remains at 100%. Verified by a path-scoped coverage run, since the repo coverage config scopes `source` to `rebar` and otherwise reports no data.\n- [ ] `make lint` passes. Verified by running it — never a bare `ruff check .`, which reports roughly 1,982 spurious errors from vendored and generated trees.\n\n## Out of scope\n\nThe two Terraform module findings from the same review, which are tracked separately. The `_metered_spend` no-data-versus-failure ambiguity, which is the blocking finding being fixed on 2984 itself. The main gate condition `if any_attached and spend < THRESHOLD_USD and not signal_unavailable:` must not gain release-on-outage behaviour — an earlier round added it, it was reverted deliberately, and that reversal stands.\n\n## Dependency\n\nChange 2984 must land first. It rewrites the surrounding no-data/unavailable handling in the same file, and defect (1) needs to adopt whatever convention it establishes.","id":"604c-ade8-0ae3-4dd7","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Bedrock spend-cap handler: CE no-data KeyError, rate limit bypassed during outage, and a self-contradicting DRY_RUN release"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1789782376063435495,"uuid":"7959f03a-8135-4cff-9d21-0fe2f9db5f8f"}